these past few III s have been kind of  disappointing for me there's been tons  of great games but there's one series  that gets me excited like no other Mass  Effect and unfortunately year after year  Bioware has been sharing very little on  the fourth entry Mass Effect Andromeda  we know very little about Andromeda  except for the fact that it takes place  in a new galaxy with a brand new suite  of characters but even still  anticipation for this new adventure is  growing so why is that what is it about  Mass Effect that gets so many of us  excited what made millions of gamers  explore the Milky Way for hours on end  that inspired thousands of cosplayers  that created an Internet movement when  the game didn't end the way many wanted  join me on a journey through the past  ten years of Mass Effect and maybe we  can find out why before we can dive into  Mass Effect we need to take a quick look  at Bioware Montreal's history and one of  its most beloved projects Bioware was  founded in 1995 by a handful of doctors  yep dr. ray mizuka dr. Greg Zeschuk and  dr. Augustine yep along with three  others created one of the most well  known RPG studios of all time the team  cut their teeth on a handful of  successful role-playing games including  Baldur's Gate Neverwinter Nights but it  wasn't until a partnership with Luca  starts at the dawn of the new millennium  that they would finally work on their  opus the game in question was something  Lucas fans have been screaming out for  years a Star Wars game where you could  create your own character command your  own spaceship explore iconic planets and  assemble a team throughout the main  quest players were able to make  decisions both good and evil which  ultimately affected the ending the name  Star Wars Knights of the Old Republic at  this time Bioware were no stranger to  RPGs but Knights of the Old Republic  marked a lot of departures for what they  typically did for starters kotor was  projected to be much shorter than most  of the developers previous games and it  would put a lot more focus on  storytelling than anything before that  it was through this that project  director Casey Hudson was able to lay  the framework for what the team was  calling Project SFX  one September morning in 2003 Hudson sat  down with the doctors and didn't just  pitch one game but three he envisioned  an ambitious trilogy where your  character actions and choices are  carried over from one game to the next  as the story goes mizuka still remembers  burning his tongue on hot soup during  Hudson's bold pitch the ambition didn't  end there either  Hudson wanted to make a game that span  an entire galaxy with a fast-paced  combat system that would rival  third-person action games the game would  be developed by the same team that  created Knights of the Old Republic he  imagined a game where players could  freely explore planets in an online  component that sounded awfully similar  to destiny or the division the original  pitch document mentions virtual  environments where you can meet other  players and trade items with them in a  dynamic in-game economy online didn't  quite make the cut but the doctors gave  Hudson the greenlight and pre-production  began the team spent the first year  putting the building blocks in place for  this massive universe Hudson and art  director Derek Watts wanted to create a  vision of the future that was clean and  slick a universe where form was just as  important as style Hudson felt that at  the time most science fiction was dark  and gritty and that Mass Effect could  touch on a style of science fiction that  hadn't been tapped into with games  drawing inspiration from futuristic art  of the 70s and 80s and films like red  planet and Solaris codename sfx soon  became Mass Effect most of us love the  sleek environments and fast paced  gameplay but the exploration was a  little more divisive you either loved it  or hated it but let's be honest you  probably hated it I'm talking about the  Mako this was your way of exploring  dozens of well rather bland and optional  worlds Mass Effect had to offer  there was a red planet a green planet a  blue planet a white one and a couple  other colors if you were lucky you'd  come across a crate filled room but  after that you've seen all the crate  filled rooms Mass Effect had to offer  unfortunately the technology at the time  made it difficult for Bioware to create  multiple and truly unique planets but  Casey Hudson and his team did manage to  pull off the combat  it may have not been as polished as  third person shooters at the time like  epics Gears of War but it was still a  huge change compared to the shooting and  Knights of the Old Republic instead of  tactically assigning characters to fight  NPCs combat was in real time and it  actually played pretty well for the time  this is partly because early on the team  decided that they would focus on range  combat for the SFX project this would  make weapon tweaking and balancing much  easier and it was another way to  disassociate their new spacefaring IP  from Star Wars players were able to  combine firearms with biotic powers tech  skills and squat abilities in order to  take down enemies from their players  could experiment with different weapons  armor mods and bullet types to make  weapons feel like their own this feature  was actually surprisingly in-depth  unfortunately a lot of it was buried  under clunky and complicated menus the  combat also evolved quite a bit over the  course of development during one of the  early trailers Bioware showed off a  system like kotor allowed players to  swap between squad members in order to  carry out different objectives and  defined enemy weak points instead  Bioware settled for a squad command  feature that would allow you to use  squad mates abilities on the fly and  tell them where to go and what to do  despite the heavy focus on action based  combat Mass Effect was still first and  foremost an RPG throughout the game you  are constantly collecting new equipment  weapons mods and armor not just for  Sheppard but your entire team Mass  Effect also had one of the most in-depth  leveling systems of the three you could  personalize every squad mate down to the  pistol he carried and the mods that were  attached to it but Mass Effect didn't  quite revolutionize third-person combat  or the RPG genre but what it may have  revolutionized is storytelling sovereign  will succeed  my way is the only way any of us will  survive the presentation took the choose  your own adventure style story to new  heights an unobtrusive radial menu would  pop up seconds before you would respond  you'd pick a shortened response and  Shepards dialog would cut in seamlessly  the camera would even move and react to  Shepards movements depending on how he'd  respond in different situations giving  it a cinematic feel and if that was in  cinematic enough Bioware added a ton of  film grain and lens flares despite its  problems mass effect proved to be an  excellent introduction into a universe  that would lay the groundwork for one of  the most ambitious trilogies ever  created gamers and critics were quick to  point out its shortcomings but what  worked worked really well  Bioware had created a huge universe  teeming with stories to tell and the  possibilities were endless  in 2009 fans were greeted with a cryptic  teaser for Bioware's follow up the  teaser listed some of Shepards  achievements from the first game and it  ended with a line of text saying he or  she was killed in action at the time we  didn't know what to make of it  could shepard really be dead by our  double down on this marketing strategy  after the teaser by concealing Shepards  face and early demos I don't think most  fans fell for this stunt but deep down I  was a bit worried that maybe the tech  wasn't quite there to make a series  where your decisions carried across  multiple games fortunately I was dead  wrong characters that had died for some  players were entirely written out into  and decisions you made in the first game  had rippling effects on the state of the  galaxy Mass Effect 2 truly felt like an  experience that had been molded by the  player but it's hard to talk about Mass  Effect 2 without acknowledging its  change in direction exploration was  toned down the Mako is cut altogether  most of the RPG elements were stripped  clean and it was also much darker the  sequel turned out to be much more action  and story focused fans were taken aback  by these changes but as it turns out  Mass Effect 2 was a much more focused  and refined version of a series gamers  were quickly falling in love with Mass  Effect 2 was an exercise in focus and  what was left intact was polished close  to perfection the lighting and textures  were better the combat felt much tighter  the characters were somehow even more  interesting and the cutscenes weren't  nearly as restrictive as before with the  cast of 12 squad mates there was no  shortage of personality aboard the  Normandy Morden was a cold but eccentric  Silurian Allegiant was a gap that could  operate independently and grunt was a  Krogan  bred for war each character had a  fleshed-out backstories and loyalty  missions where you could strengthen your  bond with that character these missions  were optional but I'd go as far to say  that Bioware told some of their finest  stories through these side quests but  one of the most startling things about  Mass Effect 2 was how much darker it was  than its predecessor human colonies were  being abducted by the thousands new  squad mates like Jack and Thane were  cold blooded killers and Shepard had  defected to a human extremist group  called Cerberus the dominating blue hues  were placed with red and the lighting  was very similar to darken war films  such as Blade Runner and if you played  Mass Effect 2 like I did Shepard was mad  really mad willing to offer you  reinstatement as a Spectre take your  offer and cram it up your ass I didn't  need your help to stop sovereign and I  don't need it now to no surprise though  Mass Effect 2 was a hit fans and critics  fell in love with the sequel and I think  it's safe to say that Mass Effect 2 is  regarded as the fan favorite among the  three the complex narrative with  multiple story and character threads  together that all factored into a  climactic suicide mission ending and if  you really [ __ ] up it wasn't out of a  question for Shepard to die for most of  us know Shepard didn't die and that's a  good thing because Shepards story wasn't  quite over yet  on March 5th 2012 at 11 p.m. I was  standing in line at my local Gamestop I  was already short on sleep after  marathoning my perfect run through  through Mass Effect 1 &amp; 2 and I was  freezing my ass off when the clock hit  midnight I picked up my copy of Mass  Effect 3's collector's edition and I  broke a few traffic laws to get home as  quickly as I could I ended up staying up  all night missing class the following  day all the Domino's had been set up  over the course of 6 years and they were  finally starting to fall decisions I had  completely forgotten about came full  circle like the rack Knight Queen on no  Varia and captainkira Hayes fate on  virmire people had vastly different  experiences all based on big and little  decisions they had made in 1 &amp; 2 Mass  Effect 3 also marked the return for some  of its RPG elements it was still very  much an action game but the leveling up  system was more in depth and weapon and  character customization were taken to  further lengths it was still a little  light on exploration but with the  impending threat of the Reapers would  there really be much time for exploring  probably not so instead Bioware took  another shot at the combat and managed  to squeeze in the last puzzle piece that  was always missing melee attacks for the  first time in this series everything  just kind of melded together I take down  an enemy's shield with disruptor rounds  command Liara to suspend an enemy with  her biotics and then I'd rush in for the  kill  unfortunately it's tough to talk about  all of Mass Effect threes achievements  without mentioning the ending  to say expectations were high is an  understatement  but the endings we got weren't quite  what fans were hoping for all your  decisions over the course of three games  boiled down to three endings to make  matters worse fans criticized these  three endings for appearing largely the  same I'm sure you remember the countless  pick your favorite color and scumbag  Shepard memes that poked fun at the  ending fans were quick to point out plot  holes and a lot of your decisions ended  up feeling inconsequential this didn't  sit well with fans and they were vocal  it didn't stop with the YouTube comments  and threatening tweets either within  days a petition popped up online  demanding Bioware fix their ending  developers were even getting death  threats one poll in particular showed  that 91% of 70,000 fans were  disappointed with the ending the team  was crushed after ten years of  development fans swore off the series  altogether just because of those last  two hours but it didn't take too long  for Bioware to set things straight a few  months after release Bioware gave fans  an extended cut of the ending that  covered up some plot holes and added a  little bit more meat to the ending but  the wound was still fresh however just  talking about Mass Effect's ending  doesn't do Mass Effect 3 justice the  ending may not have taken into account  your previous choices but the entire  game did I had three different saves  prepared going into Mass Effect 3 a  paragon save or renegades save and a  saver I did whatever I wanted to and  each playthrough felt different missions  would vary characters will be absent in  some playthroughs and relationships are  dramatically different and now that the  ending wounds have mostly healed it's  easy to see how impressive the Mass  Effect trilogy is Hudson and Bioware's  high ambitions were met and after the  beloved Citadel DLC the studio took a  well-deserved break from the French  so here we are it's 2016 and there's a  new Mass Effect game deep in development  the game was announced shortly after  Mass Effect 3 but since then the studio  has been tight-lipped apart from a  trailer and some behind-the-scenes  documentaries we still don't really know  what this game will be a few things are  for certain though Mass Effect Andromeda  will take place in the you guessed it  Andromeda galaxy based on rumors and  leaks it sounds like the new protagonist  will be tasked with colonizing habitable  planets in the Andromeda galaxy it also  looks like the Mako is making a return  and that can only mean one thing  exploration based on the trailer we saw  at e3 last year there should be dozens  maybe even hundreds of planets to  explore there's even some rumors  circulating that you'll be able to  seamlessly pilot your spaceship between  planets within a dense galaxy even with  this it's still hard to nail down  exactly what Mass Effect Andromeda will  be at this point but based on everything  we've heard so far this sounds like the  Mass Effect game Bioware and fans  envisioned with the first one for me  personally I hope Bioware does away with  all the grand and epic storylines  the best part of the Mass Effect games  have always been the characters and  their relationships players were able to  forge with them I don't want to save the  galaxy again I don't even want to save  the universe I want to explore uncharted  worlds and known interesting characters  twenty years from now we're not going to  look at Mass Effect for its combat or  for its endings when we look back on  Mass Effect we're going to think about  the characters it created and the  stories it sold
 

